<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<part xml:id="curriculum-guide" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Learning Map</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      All Junior Cycle Mathematics learning outcomes are listed here.
      Throughout the book, coloured badges link back to these outcomes.
      Click any badge to see what it means in plain language alongside the official wording.
    </p>
    <p>
      HL items are Higher Level only. OL items appear on both Ordinary and Higher Level.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- UNIFYING STRAND -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <chapter xml:id="lo-unifying">
    <title>Unifying Strand</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>These outcomes apply across all four content strands. They describe how you think, reason, and communicate mathematically.</p>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-1">
      <title>U.1: Explore Approaches</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Talk about a problem and explore different ways of solving it, explaining your thinking.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Discuss a problem and explore different approaches, explaining their reasoning.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-2">
      <title>U.2: Represent Mathematically</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Turn a real situation into mathematics using the right concepts, operations, and representations.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Represent a problem mathematically, making use of appropriate concepts, operations, and representations.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-3">
      <title>U.3: Select Methods</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Choose the right methods and procedures for a given task.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Select appropriate methods and procedures to accomplish a given task.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-4">
      <title>U.4: Estimate and Verify</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use estimation to predict results and then check whether your answer is reasonable.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Use the results of computation or estimation to predict and verify outcomes.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-5">
      <title>U.5: Evaluate Solutions</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Look at your answer critically — does it make sense? Compare different methods.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Evaluate solutions and compare different methods.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-6">
      <title>U.6: Find Patterns</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Spot patterns in data and make predictions. Then test your predictions.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Identify patterns in data, and make predictions and test them.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-7">
      <title>U.7: Justify Conclusions</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Back up your conclusions with evidence and clear arguments.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Justify conclusions using evidence and sound arguments.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-8">
      <title>U.8: Communicate Mathematics</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Express your mathematical thinking clearly, both in speech and in writing.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Communicate mathematics effectively, verbally and in writing.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-9">
      <title>U.9: Make Connections</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Link ideas within mathematics and connect mathematics to real-world situations.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Make connections both within mathematics and between mathematics and the real world.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-10">
      <title>U.10: Interpret Results</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Take your mathematical answer and explain what it means in the original context.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Interpret results in the context of the original problem.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-11">
      <title>U.11: Relevance of Mathematics</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Recognise how mathematics shows up in everyday life.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> See the relevance of mathematics to their everyday lives.</p>
    </section>

  </chapter>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- NUMBER STRAND -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <chapter xml:id="lo-number">
    <title>Number Strand</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Number outcomes cover natural numbers, integers, rationals, operations, estimation, indices, measurement, and finance.</p>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-1">
      <title>N.1: Number Systems</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Understand natural numbers <m>\mathbb{N}</m>, integers <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>, and rational numbers <m>\mathbb{Q}</m>.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate the representation of numbers and arithmetic operations.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-2">
      <title>N.2: Number Line</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Place and read numbers on a number line, including integers and fractions.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Represent the operations of addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division in ℕ, ℤ, and ℚ using models including the number line.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-3">
      <title>N.3: Factors, Multiples, Primes</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Find factors and multiples of numbers. Identify prime numbers. Find HCF and LCM.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate the properties of arithmetic: commutative, associative, and distributive.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-4">
      <title>N.4: Prime Factorisation</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Break any whole number down into its prime factors. Use factor trees and index notation.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Express numbers in terms of their prime factors.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-5">
      <title>N.5: Order of Operations</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Apply BIMDAS correctly — Brackets, Indices, Multiplication/Division, Addition/Subtraction.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Appreciate the order of operations, including brackets.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-6">
      <title>N.6: Properties of Arithmetic</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use the commutative, associative, and distributive laws to simplify calculations.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate the properties of arithmetic: commutative, associative, and distributive.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-7">
      <title>N.7: Integers</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Add, subtract, multiply, and divide positive and negative numbers confidently.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate models such as the number line to illustrate operations in ℤ.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-8">
      <title>N.8: Fractions, Decimals, Percentages</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Convert between fractions, decimals, and percentages and use them interchangeably to compare proportions.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Use the equivalence of fractions, decimals, and percentages to compare proportions.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-9">
      <title>N.9: Ratio and Proportion</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Understand and apply ratio and proportion to solve problems.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Consolidate the idea of a relationship between ratio and proportion.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-10">
      <title>N.10: Percentages</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Calculate percentages of amounts, percentage increase, and percentage decrease.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Solve problems involving percentage.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-11">
      <title>N.11: Profit, Loss, Discount, VAT</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Solve financial problems involving profit, loss, discount, and VAT.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Solve problems that involve finding profit or loss, discount, VAT.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-12">
      <title>N.12: Compound Interest (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Calculate compound interest and solve related financial problems. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Solve problems involving compound interest.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-13">
      <title>N.13: Index Notation</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use and apply the rules of indices: <m>a^m \times a^n = a^{m+n}</m>, <m>a^m \div a^n = a^{m-n}</m>, <m>(a^m)^n = a^{mn}</m>.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Use and apply the rules of indices.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-14">
      <title>N.14: Zero and Negative Indices</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Understand that <m>a^0 = 1</m> and that negative indices mean reciprocals.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Use and apply the rules of indices.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-15">
      <title>N.15: Standard Form</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Write very large or very small numbers in the form <m>a \times 10^n</m> where <m>1 \leq a \lt 10</m>.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Express non-zero positive rational numbers in the form <m>a \times 10^n</m>.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-16">
      <title>N.16: Calculations in Standard Form</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Multiply and divide numbers written in standard form.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Perform arithmetic operations on numbers in standard form.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-17">
      <title>N.17: Rounding and Accuracy</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Round answers to the required number of decimal places or significant figures.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Present numerical answers to the degree of accuracy specified.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-18">
      <title>N.18: Estimation</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Make sensible estimates and justify whether your answers are reasonable.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Make and justify estimates and approximations of calculations.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-19">
      <title>N.19: Percentage Error (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Calculate the percentage error between an estimated and actual value. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Calculate percentage error.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-20">
      <title>N.20: Units and Measurement</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use appropriate units for measuring. Convert between units including time, length, mass, and capacity.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Calculate, interpret, and apply units of measure and time.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-21">
      <title>N.21: Perimeter and Area</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Find the perimeter and area of rectangles, triangles, circles, and other plane shapes.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Find the perimeter and area of plane figures.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-22">
      <title>N.22: Composite Areas</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Find the area of shapes made from combinations of simpler shapes.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Find the area of figures made from combinations of shapes.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-23">
      <title>N.23: Volume and Surface Area</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Find the volume and surface area of rectangular solids, cylinders, and spheres.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Find the volume and surface area of rectangular solids, cylinders, and spheres.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-24">
      <title>N.24: Scale Diagrams</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Draw and interpret diagrams drawn to scale.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Draw and interpret scaled diagrams.</p>
    </section>

  </chapter>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- GEOMETRY AND TRIGONOMETRY -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <chapter xml:id="lo-geometry">
    <title>Geometry and Trigonometry</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Geometry outcomes cover constructions, angle properties, triangles, coordinate geometry, trigonometry, and transformations.</p>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-1">
      <title>GT.1: Construct a Line Segment</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use a ruler and compass to construct a line segment of a given length.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Perform the constructions specified in the syllabus.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-2">
      <title>GT.2: Bisect an Angle</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use a compass to bisect any angle accurately.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Perform the constructions specified in the syllabus.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-3">
      <title>GT.3: Perpendicular Bisector</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Construct the perpendicular bisector of a line segment using a compass.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Perform the constructions specified in the syllabus.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-4">
      <title>GT.4: Perpendicular Through a Point</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Construct a line perpendicular to a given line through a specified point.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Perform the constructions specified in the syllabus.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-5">
      <title>GT.5: Angles at a Point and on a Line</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Calculate angles at a point (360°), on a straight line (180°), and vertically opposite angles.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate angle properties.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-6">
      <title>GT.6: Angle Sum in Triangles and Quadrilaterals</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> The angles in a triangle add to 180°; in a quadrilateral, 360°.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate angle properties of triangles and quadrilaterals.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-7">
      <title>GT.7: Parallel Lines and Transversals</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Find angles formed when a line crosses two parallel lines: alternate, co-interior, corresponding.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate transversals and parallel lines.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-8">
      <title>GT.8: Isosceles and Equilateral Triangles</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Know and use the special angle and side properties of isosceles and equilateral triangles.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate properties of triangles.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-9">
      <title>GT.9: Pythagoras' Theorem (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> In a right-angled triangle, <m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>. Use this to find missing sides. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate the theorem of Pythagoras and its applications.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-10">
      <title>GT.10: Converse of Pythagoras (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> If <m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>, the triangle is right-angled. Use this to test triangles. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate the converse of the theorem of Pythagoras.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-11">
      <title>GT.11: Circle Theorems (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Investigate and apply theorems about angles in circles. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate theorems about angles in circles.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-12">
      <title>GT.12: Proof Vocabulary (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use and understand the terms: theorem, proof, axiom, corollary, converse. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Use the following terms: theorem, proof, axiom, corollary, converse.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-13">
      <title>GT.13: The Coordinate Plane</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Plot and read points on the <m>(x, y)</m> coordinate plane.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Use co-ordinate geometry techniques.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-14">
      <title>GT.14: Midpoint of a Segment</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Find the midpoint of a line segment using the midpoint formula.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Find the midpoint of a line segment.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-15">
      <title>GT.15: Distance Between Two Points</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Calculate the distance between two points using the distance formula.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Calculate the distance between two points.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-16">
      <title>GT.16: Slope of a Line</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Calculate slope as rise over run and interpret what it means in a context.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Calculate and interpret slope.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-17">
      <title>GT.17: Trigonometric Ratios</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use sin, cos, and tan to find missing sides and angles in right-angled triangles.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Use trigonometric ratios to solve right-angled triangle problems.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-18">
      <title>GT.18: Trigonometry Applications</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Apply trigonometric ratios to real-world problems such as heights and distances.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Apply trigonometric ratios to real-world contexts.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-19">
      <title>GT.19: Area Using Sine (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Find the area of any triangle using the formula <m>\frac{1}{2}ab\sin C</m>. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Find the area of a triangle using <m>\frac{1}{2}ab \sin C</m>.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-20">
      <title>GT.20: Transformations</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Recognise and perform translations, reflections, and rotations of shapes.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Recognise and perform transformations.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-21">
      <title>GT.21: Axes of Symmetry</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Find and draw the axes of symmetry in simple shapes.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Locate axes of symmetry in simple shapes.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-22">
      <title>GT.22: Enlargements</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Enlarge shapes by a given scale factor and understand what changes (and what does not).</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate enlargements.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-gt-23">
      <title>GT.23: Enlargement and Area</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Understand how enlargement by scale factor <m>k</m> multiplies area by <m>k^2</m>.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate enlargements and their effect on area.</p>
    </section>

  </chapter>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- ALGEBRA AND FUNCTIONS -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <chapter xml:id="lo-algebra">
    <title>Algebra and Functions</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Algebra outcomes cover expressions, equations, inequalities, sequences, functions, and graphing.</p>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-1">
      <title>AF.1: Variables</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use letters to represent unknown quantities and variable values.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Use letter symbols for numeric quantities.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-2">
      <title>AF.2: Evaluate Expressions</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Substitute given values into algebraic expressions and calculate the result.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Evaluate expressions given the value of the variables.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-3">
      <title>AF.3: Simplify Expressions</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Collect like terms to simplify algebraic expressions.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Simplify expressions.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-4">
      <title>AF.4: Expand Brackets</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Expand expressions of the form <m>a(b + c) = ab + ac</m>.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Expand and simplify expressions.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-5">
      <title>AF.5: Expand Binomials</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Expand the product of two brackets, e.g. <m>(x + a)(x + b)</m>.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Expand products of binomials.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-6">
      <title>AF.6: Factorise — Common Factor</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Take the highest common factor outside a bracket: <m>ab + ac = a(b + c)</m>.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Factorise expressions.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-7">
      <title>AF.7: Factorise Quadratics (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Factorise expressions of the form <m>x^2 + bx + c</m> and <m>ax^2 + bx + c</m>. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Factorise expressions of order 2.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-8">
      <title>AF.8: Rearrange Formulae</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Change the subject of a formula by rearranging it.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Rearrange formulae.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-9">
      <title>AF.9: Solve Linear Equations</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Solve equations of the form <m>ax + b = c</m>.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Solve first-degree equations in one variable.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-10">
      <title>AF.10: Equations with Unknown on Both Sides</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Solve equations where the unknown appears on both sides, e.g. <m>3x + 1 = x + 7</m>.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Solve first-degree equations with the unknown on both sides.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-11">
      <title>AF.11: Equations with Brackets and Fractions</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Solve linear equations that involve brackets and/or fractions.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Solve equations involving brackets and fractions.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-12">
      <title>AF.12: Form and Solve Equations</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Read a word problem, write it as an equation, and solve it.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Solve problems involving setting up and solving linear equations.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-13">
      <title>AF.13: Simultaneous Equations</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Solve two equations in two unknowns, both graphically and algebraically.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Solve simultaneous linear equations with two unknowns.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-14">
      <title>AF.14: Solve Quadratics by Factorising (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Solve quadratic equations by factorising and setting each factor equal to zero. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Solve quadratic equations by factorising.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-15">
      <title>AF.15: Quadratic Formula (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use the quadratic formula <m>x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}</m> to solve quadratic equations. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Solve quadratic equations using the quadratic formula.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-16">
      <title>AF.16: Linear Inequalities</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Solve linear inequalities and show the solution set on a number line.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Solve linear inequalities.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-17">
      <title>AF.17: Linear Patterns</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Continue and describe linear patterns using words, tables, and diagrams.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate patterns and relationships.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-18">
      <title>AF.18: Arithmetic Sequences — nth Term</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Find the general (nth) term of an arithmetic sequence.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Find the general term of an arithmetic sequence.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-19">
      <title>AF.19: Quadratic and Geometric Sequences (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Recognise and work with quadratic and geometric sequences. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Recognise quadratic and geometric sequences.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-20">
      <title>AF.20: Functions — Definition</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Understand that a function assigns exactly one output to each input.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Recognise that a function assigns a unique output to a given input.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-21">
      <title>AF.21: Function Notation</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use <m>f(x)</m> notation and evaluate functions for given inputs.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Use function notation.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-22">
      <title>AF.22: Represent Functions</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Represent functions using tables, mapping diagrams, and graphs.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Represent functions in different ways.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-23">
      <title>AF.23: Inputs and Outputs</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Given an input, find the output. Given an output, find the input.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Determine inputs and outputs of functions.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-24">
      <title>AF.24: Graph Linear Functions</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Plot linear functions <m>y = mx + c</m> and read information from the graph.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Graph linear functions.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-25">
      <title>AF.25: Slope and Intercept</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Interpret the slope as rate of change and the <m>y</m>-intercept as the starting value.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Interpret the slope and y-intercept of linear functions.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-26">
      <title>AF.26: Graph Quadratic Functions (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Plot quadratic functions and identify the vertex, axis of symmetry, and roots. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Graph quadratic functions.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-27">
      <title>AF.27: Use Graphs to Solve Equations</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use graphical methods to solve equations of the form <m>f(x) = g(x)</m>.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Use graphical methods to solve equations.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-28">
      <title>AF.28: Interpret Graphs</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Read and interpret graphs that represent real-world situations.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Interpret graphs in real-world contexts.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-29">
      <title>AF.29: Graphs and Stories</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Match graphs to verbal descriptions of situations, and vice versa.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Connect graphs and real-world phenomena.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-af-30">
      <title>AF.30: Quantity and Rate of Change</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Describe both the quantity and the rate of change shown in a graph.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Describe quantity and rate of change from graphs.</p>
    </section>

  </chapter>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- STATISTICS AND PROBABILITY -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <chapter xml:id="lo-statistics">
    <title>Statistics and Probability</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Statistics and probability outcomes cover listing outcomes, calculating probability, collecting data, representing data, and interpreting results.</p>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-1">
      <title>SP.1: List Outcomes</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> List all possible outcomes of an experiment systematically (e.g. using a table or tree diagram).</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> List all possible outcomes of an experiment.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-2">
      <title>SP.2: Probability Scale</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Understand that probability is a number between 0 (impossible) and 1 (certain).</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Recognise that probability is a measure on a scale of 0–1.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-3">
      <title>SP.3: Experimental Probability</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Estimate probability by doing an experiment and recording relative frequencies.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Estimate probabilities from experimental data.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-4">
      <title>SP.4: Long-Run Relative Frequency</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Understand that as more trials are done, experimental probability gets closer to theoretical probability.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Associate probability with long-run relative frequency.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-5">
      <title>SP.5: Calculate Probability</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use the formula: <m>P(\text{event}) = \frac{\text{favourable outcomes}}{\text{total outcomes}}</m>.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Apply the principle that probability = favourable outcomes / total outcomes.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-6">
      <title>SP.6: Probability in Practical Contexts</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Calculate probabilities in practical settings: coins, dice, spinners, cards.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Calculate probabilities in practical contexts.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-7">
      <title>SP.7: Sample Spaces for Two Events (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Construct a sample space (table or list) for two independent events. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Construct sample spaces for two independent events.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-8">
      <title>SP.8: Combined Events (HL)</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Find the probability of combined events using addition and multiplication rules. [Higher Level]</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Find the probability of combined events.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-9">
      <title>SP.9: Statistical Questions</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Formulate a question that can be answered using data.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Formulate one or more questions that can be answered with data.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-10">
      <title>SP.10: Data Collection</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Plan and carry out a data collection using a Simple Random Sample.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Select a sample (Simple Random Sample).</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-11">
      <title>SP.11: Avoiding Bias</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Understand why samples must be representative and how bias distorts conclusions.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Recognise the importance of representativeness so as to avoid biased samples.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-12">
      <title>SP.12: Generate or Source Data</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Collect your own data or find suitable data from external sources, including the internet.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Generate data, or source data from other sources including the internet.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-13">
      <title>SP.13: Choose Appropriate Displays</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Select the right type of graph or display for your data and purpose.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Select appropriate methods to represent and describe the sample.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-14">
      <title>SP.14: Draw Statistical Graphs</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Construct bar charts, pie charts, line plots, histograms, and stem-and-leaf plots.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Use pie charts, bar charts, line plots, histograms, stem and leaf plots.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-15">
      <title>SP.15: Mean, Median, Mode</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Calculate and interpret the mean, median, and mode of a data set.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Use mean, median, and mode to describe central tendency.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-16">
      <title>SP.16: Range</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Calculate the range of a data set as a measure of spread.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Use range to describe variability.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-17">
      <title>SP.17: Interpret Graphical Summaries</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Read and interpret graphs and charts to draw conclusions from data.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Interpret graphical summaries of data.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-18">
      <title>SP.18: Evaluate Displays</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Assess how effectively a particular display represents the data.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Evaluate the effectiveness of different displays.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-19">
      <title>SP.19: Sampling Variability</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Understand that different samples give different results, and that this affects conclusions.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Recognise how sampling variability influences the use of sample information.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-sp-20">
      <title>SP.20: Misconceptions and Misuse</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Identify ways statistics can be misleading or misused, and critically evaluate statistical claims.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Recognise misconceptions and misuses of statistics.</p>
    </section>

  </chapter>

</part>
